> I am writing up a methods appendix for a report. The first part of this section discusses risk modeling:
> ```
> \subsection{Risk modeling}
> 
> We assess coastal impacts by combining probabilistic projections of global mean sea-level rise (GSLR) with two global, engineering–economic coastal impact models: CIAM \citep{diaz2016estimating} and DIVA \citepalias{lincked2019d23}. Both models represent coastal processes, assets at risk, and adaptation responses at a high spatial resolution. Since realised adaptation levels are uncertain, we treat the difference between “no/ad hoc adaptation” and “optimal (least-cost) adaptation” cases as an explicit source of uncertainty in future damages.
> 
> Global sea-level rise is projected using the semi-empirical model of \citet{kopp2016temperature}, driven by temperatures from the FaIR simple climate model. To propagate uncertainty consistently, each FaIR Monte Carlo temperature trajectory is randomly matched to a Monte Carlo realization from the HadCRUT-based historical temperature ensemble used by \citet{kopp2016temperature}. Kopp et al. provide two separate calibrations (based on different observational datasets), each with its own parametric uncertainty; for each run we randomly draw from one of these calibrations and its parameter distribution. This yields a joint ensemble of temperature and sea-level trajectories.
> 
> To supply CIAM with representative low, central, and high sea-level scenarios, we re-sample this ensemble via bootstrap. In each of 1000 bootstrap iterations, we identify the model run whose 2100 GSLR outcome is closest to a target quantile (5th, 50th, or 95th percentile), thus constructing three internally consistent time series representing low-, median-, and high-end GSLR under the FaIR-driven temperature uncertainty and the Kopp et al. semi-empirical relationship. These trajectories are used as inputs to CIAM for subsequent impact estimation.
> 
> CIAM is built on the DIVA coastal segmentation, which partitions the global coastline into 12{,}148 segments with relatively homogeneous physical characteristics (median segment length about 18\,km). The UK coastline is represented by 402 of these segments. For each segment, CIAM combines physical attributes (e.g., coastline length, elevation profile, storm surge statistics, wetland extent) and socio-economic attributes (e.g., income, population density, capital stock, land value) with cost data for coastal protection and retreat. Physical attributes are taken from DIVA, while socio-economic variables and cost parameters draw on the data sources documented in \citet{diaz2016estimating}. CIAM operates in decadal time steps, initialised in 2000 and extending to 2200; we focus on the period up to 2100. Model costs are denominated in 2010 US dollars and are converted to 2020 US dollars using a 20\% decadal inflation factor.
> 
> Impacts in CIAM are decomposed into five cost categories at the segment level:
> (i) protection costs (e.g., constructing and maintaining sea walls and dikes),
> (ii) retreat costs (relocating population and mobile capital, and demolishing immobile capital),
> (iii) inundation costs (permanent loss of land and abandoned immobile capital),
> (iv) wetland costs (lost wetland ecosystem services), and
> (v) flood costs (expected storm-surge damages to capital and expected mortality).
> The total cost of sea-level rise for a segment is the discounted sum of these components over the planning horizon.
> 
> Adaptation behaviour is represented by a segment-level social planner who chooses an adaptation strategy $s$ to minimise the discounted sum of protection, retreat, inundation, wetland, and flood costs over an adaptation planning period. Formally, for each segment and planning period, the planner solves
> \[
> \min_s \sum_{t \in \Delta t} \frac{1}{(1+r)^t} \Bigl(
> \text{ProtectionCost}_{s t} +
> \text{RetreatCost}_{s t} +
> \text{InundationCost}_{s t} +
> \text{WetlandCost}_{s t} +
> \text{FloodCost}_{s t}
> \Bigr),
> \]
> where $\Delta t$ contains decadal time steps $t$ within the current planning period and $r$ is the discount rate. Under the “no adaptation” case, protection is never undertaken and retreat is chosen myopically within each time step in response to realised sea-level rise. Under “optimal adaptation”, the planner can undertake both protection and retreat once per planning period, with perfect foresight over the current and next planning periods. CIAM solves this optimisation independently for each of the 12{,}148 segments and sequentially across planning periods (default periods are 2000; 2010--2040; 2050--2090; 2100--2140; 2150--2180; 2190--2200), implying a limited-horizon but forward-looking adaptation strategy.
> 
> DIVA-based results are used analogously, with impacts computed across similar categories of coastal damage and adaptation and aggregated to the national level for the UK. In both modelling frameworks, we extract annual monetary costs by segment (or region), aggregate them to country-level damages, and express outcomes in billions of pounds per year. To represent adaptation uncertainty, we treat the no-/limited-adaptation and optimal-adaptation runs as bounding cases. For each model, we perform Monte Carlo sampling over these adaptation cases and over GSLR trajectories, yielding a distribution of possible national coastal damage outcomes.
> 
> Finally, we convert the resulting coastal damages into percentage losses of UK GDP by dividing the annual monetary losses by a no-damage GDP baseline for the corresponding year. Where needed, we harmonise currencies, price levels, and baseline GDP paths to be consistent with the rest of the sectoral impact analysis. Further details of the uncertainty propagation, currency conversion, and model-combination procedures applied across all sectors are provided in \citet{rising2026comprehensive}, and are not repeated here.
