> ```latex
> \subsection{Adaptation modelling}
> 
> To assess the effectiveness of incremental coastal adaptation investment, we estimate a reduced-form relationship between sea-level rise, adaptation effort, and total coastal damages. Starting from the national and sub-national results produced by the coastal impact models, we construct a panel of decadal damages under two adaptation cases (no or ad hoc adaptation; optimal adaptation) and two emissions pathways (SSP1-2.6 and SSP3-7.0), for each UK coastal area $i$ and year $t$ up to 2100. For each area and year, we compute mean total damages and associated uncertainty under both adaptation cases, and we separate the protection component of costs from the remaining damage categories. These coastal impact outputs are then matched with probabilistic projections of global mean sea-level rise (central quantile) for the corresponding scenario and year.
> 
> For each coastal area $i$, we focus on those locations where sea-level rise, protection spending, and the difference between optimal and no-adaptation damages are all non-trivial, so that adaptation effects can be identified. For such locations, we pool observations across emissions pathways and years and re-express total damages $D_{i t}$, protection investment $I_{i t}$, and sea-level rise $\Delta SL_{t}$ in normalised form to improve numerical conditioning. We then fit, using Bayesian inference, a parametric damage function of the form
> \[
> D_{i t} \approx \beta_{0,i} + \beta_{1,i} \Delta SL_{t} + \beta_{2,i} \Delta SL_{t}^2,
> \]
> augmented by a multiplicative adaptation term that captures how cumulative local protection effort reduces damages. Specifically, we assume that cumulative adaptation effort at time $t$ in area $i$ evolves according to
> \[
> A_{i t} = I_{i t} + \delta_i A_{i, t-1},
> \]
> where $\delta_i \in (0,1)$ is an area-specific parameter governing the persistence of adaptation benefits over time. Conditional on $A_{i t}$, residual damages are assumed to scale as
> \[
> D_{i t}^{\text{adapted}} = D_{i t}^{\text{no-adapt}} \exp\left( \eta_i \frac{A_{i t}}{\text{scale}} \right),
> \]
> where $D_{i t}^{\text{no-adapt}}$ denotes the counterfactual no-adaptation damages implied by the quadratic baseline, $\eta_i \leq 0$ is an area-specific adaptation effectiveness parameter, and the denominator rescales adaptation effort into billions of pounds to aid interpretation. The model includes an additive error term with area-specific scale $\sigma_i$, and is estimated jointly for all years and adaptation cases, using priors on coefficients and noise terms that favour smooth damage responses but allow for substantial heterogeneity across coastal areas (following weakly informative-prior recommendations in Bayesian applied work; \citealp{gelman2008weakly} (Bayesian regression practice)).
> 
> For each coastal area $i$, we draw an ensemble of Monte Carlo realisations $m$ of $(\beta_{0,i},\beta_{1,i},\beta_{2,i},\eta_i,\delta_i,\sigma_i)$ from the fitted posterior distribution. These draws are then used to simulate the discounted benefit of a marginal, centrally financed adaptation programme, defined as an additional constant national expenditure stream on coastal protection over 2025--2075. In each Monte Carlo realisation, the national expenditure is allocated across coastal areas in proportion to their location-specific present-value marginal damage reduction, such that regions with higher estimated returns receive larger shares of the budget. Given an assumed social discount rate and a baseline sea-level trajectory under SSP3-7.0, we propagate the additional expenditure forward in time using the persistence parameter $\delta_i$, compute the resulting reduction in discounted damages relative to the no-additional-investment counterfactual, and thereby obtain an implied social benefit of adaptation per pound spent for each area and realisation, denoted $B_{i m}$.
> 
> The resulting distribution of $B_{i m}$ is summarised at the county level by averaging across Monte Carlo draws and reporting the mean present-value benefit of one pound of additional coastal adaptation spending, expressed as a benefit--cost ratio. These ratios are then mapped across UK counties to provide a spatial profile of adaptation effectiveness. Figure~\ref{fig:slr-dDdI-ADM2} shows the resulting distribution of the social benefit of adaptation investment across counties, with values plotted on a logarithmic scale. This figure highlights substantial spatial heterogeneity in the expected returns to coastal adaptation, reflecting differences in exposure, baseline risk, and the scope for cost-effective protection.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{figures/adapt/slr/slr-dDdI-ADM2.pdf}
>   \caption{Estimated social benefit of additional coastal adaptation investment (present-value benefit per pound spent) across UK counties, based on the reduced-form adaptation model described in the text.}
>   \label{fig:slr-dDdI-ADM2}
> \end{figure}
> ```
