> ```latex
> \subsection{Variability and extreme events}
> 
> To capture the role of interannual variability and rare extremes in coastal damages, we extend the reduced-form adaptation model to a stochastic process for annual event losses. For each UK coastal area $i$, we take the posterior draws of the segment-specific damage-function parameters and combine them with probabilistic sea-level projections to generate Monte Carlo realisations $m$ of annual expected damages over 2025--2075, under both the Most Likely and High Risk climate scenarios.
> 
> For each year $t$, we first construct an ensemble of global mean sea-level anomalies by sampling from the relevant quantile curves of the sea-level projection model. Under the Most Likely case, we interpolate around the central quantile to obtain a representative distribution of sea-level outcomes, whereas under the High Risk case we sample directly from the full scenario-specific quantile function (here SSP3-7.0). For each Monte Carlo draw $m$, this yields a realisation of sea-level rise $\Delta SL_{t m}$, which is combined with the corresponding draw of the reduced-form coefficients to obtain an implied segment-level expected annual damage,
> \[
> \text{EAD}_{i t m}^{\ast} = \beta_{0,i m} + \beta_{1,i m} \Delta SL_{t m} + \beta_{2,i m} \Delta SL_{t m}^2.
> \]
> To avoid non-physical negative values, we bound expected damages below by the estimated noise scale, and denote the resulting non-negative annual expectation by $\text{EAD}_{i t m}$.
> 
> Conditional on $\text{EAD}_{i t m}$, we then represent realised annual coastal losses as a stochastic outcome from an exponential distribution with mean $\text{EAD}_{i t m}$. This choice yields a right-skewed distribution of annual damages with occasional large realisations, consistent with the dominance of infrequent surge and storm events in coastal loss statistics. For each area $i$, year $t$, and Monte Carlo draw $m$, we simulate a single exponential draw to obtain a realisation of annual damage $L_{i t m}$. Aggregating across Monte Carlo draws, we compute for each segment and year the mean $\text{EAD}_{i t} = \mathbb{E}_m[L_{i t m}]$ and associated central credible interval, under both the Most Likely and High Risk scenarios.
> 
> To approximate multi-year extreme losses, we further analyse the High Risk scenario by examining decadal blocks centred on selected years (2030, 2040, 2050). For each central year $\tau \in \{2030, 2040, 2050\}$ and each Monte Carlo draw $m$, we identify the single year $t$ within the surrounding decade $[\tau, \tau+9]$ that yields the largest total national damage. This is done by summing $L_{i t m}$ across all coastal areas $i$ for each year within the decade, and selecting the year with the maximum aggregate loss. For each segment $i$ and Monte Carlo draw $m$, we then retain the corresponding annual loss in that worst national year, $L^{\text{extreme}}_{i \tau m}$. The distribution of these decadal maxima is summarised, for each segment and central year $\tau$, by its mean and central credible interval, and reported as a proxy for 1-in-200-year coastal loss events at the segment level.
> 
> The resulting time series of mean expected annual damages and decadal extreme losses are used in two ways. First, they are aggregated across segments to obtain national profiles of EAD and representative rare-event damages under each scenario. Second, we retain the segment-level series to illustrate the diversity of temporal patterns across UK coastal areas, including the relative magnitude of typical annual damages versus rare, high-loss years. Figure~\ref{fig:slr-dataset-byseg} shows, for each segment, the evolution of expected annual damages under the Most Likely and High Risk scenarios and the corresponding segment-level contributions to the decadal extreme events.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/adapt/slr/slr-dataset-byseg.pdf}
>   \caption{Time series of expected annual coastal damages and decadal extreme-event damages by coastal segment. Lines show expected annual damages under the Most Likely and High Risk climate scenarios, while red markers indicate segment-level damages in the nationally worst year within each selected decade under the High Risk case.}
>   \label{fig:slr-dataset-byseg}
> \end{figure}
> ```
